# Logic Query Engine Architecture


## Overview


Logic Query Engine is designed as a simplified relational database system.

The architecture separates each stage of query processing into independent modules.


The system follows:


```
Query Input

↓

Lexical Analysis

↓

Parsing

↓

Abstract Syntax Tree

↓

Query Planning

↓

Optimisation

↓

Execution

↓

Result
```


---

# Component Architecture


## 1. Lexer


File:

```
src/lexer.c
```


Responsibility:

Convert raw query text into tokens.


Example:


Input:

```
SELECT name WHERE grade > 80
```


Output:


```
SELECT

IDENTIFIER(name)

WHERE

IDENTIFIER(grade)

>

NUMBER(80)
```


The lexer performs the first stage of query processing.


---

# 2. Parser


Files:


```
src/parser.c

include/parser.h
```


Responsibility:


Transform tokens into a structured query representation.


Pipeline:


```
Tokens

↓

Parser

↓

Query Object

↓

Abstract Syntax Tree
```


---

# 3. Abstract Syntax Tree


Files:


```
src/ast.c

include/ast.h
```


The AST represents the logical meaning of the query.


Example:


```
SELECT

|

PROJECTION(name)

|

WHERE

|

grade > 80
```


The tree structure allows future support for:

- multiple conditions
- joins
- complex expressions


---

# 4. Query Planner


Files:


```
src/planner.c
```


Responsibility:


Convert the AST into an executable logical plan.


Example:


```
AST

↓

Selection Operation

↓

Projection Operation

↓

Execution Plan
```


---

# 5. Optimiser


Files:


```
src/optimiser.c
```


Responsibility:


Analyse the query plan.


Current functionality:

- cost estimation framework
- optimisation pipeline


Future improvements:

- predicate pushdown
- join optimisation
- indexing decisions


---

# 6. Storage Layer


Files:


```
src/storage.c
```


Responsibility:


Load external data into memory.


Current format:


```
CSV
```

Example:


```
Alice,95
Bob,72
Charlie,88
```


Converted into:


```
Relation

↓

Tuple Collection
```


---

# 7. Relation Engine


Files:


```
src/relation.c
```


Database theory model:


```
Relation = Set of Tuples
```


Example:


```
Student(Name,Grade)
```


Stored as:


```
{
(Alice,95),

(Bob,72),

(Charlie,88)
}
```


---

# 8. Execution Engine


Files:


```
src/executor.c
```


Responsibility:


Execute relational operations.


Implemented operations:


## Selection


Filtering tuples:


```
σ condition(Relation)
```


Example:


```
grade > 80
```


---

## Projection


Selecting attributes:


```
π attribute(Relation)
```


Example:


```
name
```


---

# Complete Execution Flow


Example:


Query:


```
SELECT name WHERE grade > 80
```


Execution:


```
User Query

↓

Lexer

↓

Tokens

↓

Parser

↓

AST

↓

Logical Plan

↓

Optimizer

↓

Executor

↓

Relation Result
```


---

# Design Principles


## Modularity


Each component has one responsibility.


This improves:

- maintainability
- testing
- future expansion


---

## Extensibility


The architecture allows future database features:


- JOIN processing
- indexes
- transactions
- query caching
- persistent storage


---

# Relationship To Industry Databases


Professional systems such as:

- PostgreSQL
- SQLite
- MySQL


use similar conceptual stages:


```
Parser

↓

Planner

↓

Optimizer

↓

Executor

↓

Storage
```


This project implements a simplified educational version of that architecture.


---

# Engineering Reflection


The purpose of this project is not to replace existing database systems.

The purpose is to understand the engineering principles behind them.

A strong software engineer understands not only how to use abstractions, but how those abstractions are constructed.